"""        <h2>Useful Phrases <span class="em">🧰</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">Ten phrases for any renewal, any provider, any creeping bill — broadband, insurance, the gym, the streaming pile. Say each out loud BEFORE you reveal it.</p>

        <div class="deliver"><b>Say it like you mean it.</b> One sentence in this call carries everything: "then I'd like to cancel." It only works if it's true — so before you dial, decide honestly that you'll switch if they don't move. People can hear a bluff down a phone line. They can also hear the absence of one.</div>

        <h3>📋 Opening with the facts</h3>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 1</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> your standing and the trigger, one sentence, no anger. Complete it:<br><br>"I've been with you ___ years, and my bill's just ___ ___ again."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"I've been with you eight years, and my bill's just gone up again."</b><br>The two facts that define the whole call, delivered before any feelings: how long you've paid, and what they've just done. "Again" is the quiet sting — it names the pattern, not just this year's rise. No accusation needed; the sentence is the accusation, wearing a suit.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "I've been with you ___ years, and ___ has just gone up again." Insurers, gyms, landlords, software subscriptions.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 2</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> setting the tone — this is a negotiation, not a moan. Which version?</p>
            <div class="choice">
                <div><b>A.</b> "Honestly, it's disgraceful how you treat loyal customers."</div>
                <div><b>B.</b> "I'm not ringing to complain — I'm ringing to see what you can do."</div>
                <div><b>C.</b> "I suppose there's probably nothing you can do about the price…?"</div>
            </div>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the answer</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>B — "I'm not ringing to complain — I'm ringing to see what you can do."</b><br>A puts Darren on the defensive, and defensive agents reach for the script, not the discounts. C answers itself — ask whether nothing can be done and nothing will be. B is the businesslike middle: no grievance to soothe, just a question with money in it, aimed at the one person whose screen can answer it.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "I'm not ringing to complain — I'm ringing to see what you can do." Turns every service call from therapy into commerce.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 3</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> the competitor's number, with proof attached. Complete it:<br><br>"Vodafone are offering the same thing for twenty-eight — I've got it in ___ of me."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"Vodafone are offering the same thing for twenty-eight — I've got it in front of me."</b><br>The engine of the entire call. A vague "I've seen cheaper deals" moves nobody; a named rival, a real number and "in front of me" tells the agent you're one browser tab from leaving. Do the ten minutes of homework before dialling — it's the best hourly rate you'll ever earn.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "___ are offering the same thing for ___ — I've got it in front of me." The sentence that converts research into money.</div>
        </div>

        <h3>🪜 Walking down the ladder</h3>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 4</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> receiving the first offer — credit it, and refuse it. Complete it:<br><br>"That's better — but it's still ___ than I'd pay next door."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"That's better — but it's still more than I'd pay next door."</b><br>The first offer is never the last — it exists to catch the callers who just wanted to be heard. This sentence banks the progress ("that's better") while keeping the comparison alive ("next door"). Warm, unhurried, and unmistakably not done yet. Then wait; silence does the rest.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "That's better — but it's still more than ___." Acknowledge every step; accept only the last one.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 5</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> when they defend the price with features you never use. Complete it:<br><br>"I don't need the extras — I'd rather ___ them and pay ___."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"I don't need the extras — I'd rather lose them and pay less."</b><br>The standard defence of a high price is the bundle: the SIM you never activated, the app you can't use, the magazine nobody reads. This sentence calls the bluff by inviting them to remove it all — and it usually unlocks a price they couldn't reach while the bundle stood. Trading down is a negotiating move, not a defeat.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "I don't need the extras — I'd rather lose them and pay less." Phone plans, insurance add-ons, software tiers.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 6</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> the sentence the whole call is built around. Put the pieces in order:</p>
            <div class="jumble"><span>then I'd like</span><span>the best you can do,</span><span>to cancel.</span><span>If that's really</span></div>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"If that's really the best you can do, then I'd like to cancel."</b><br>Calm, conditional, and final — no raised voice, because the words carry the weight. "If that's really the best" gives them one last honest chance, and "I'd like to cancel" opens the door on the screen where the deepest discounts live. Say it slowly. And only say it if it's true — the follow-through ("the install team can come Thursday") is what proves it isn't theatre.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "If that's really the best you can do, then I'd like to ___." Cancel, switch, look elsewhere — the calm walk-away.</div>
        </div>

        <h3>🔍 Checking the deal before it's a deal</h3>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 7</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> finding the trapdoor in the shiny price. Complete it:<br><br>"Is that the price for ___, or does it ___ again after a year?"</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"Is that the price for good, or does it jump again after a year?"</b><br><b>For good</b> = permanently. Teaser pricing is the industry's favourite trick — the discount that quietly expires while the contract doesn't. One blunt question drags the reversion date into daylight, and an honest answer ("fixed for eighteen months, then it reverts") lets you diarise the next call instead of being ambushed by the next rise.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "Is that for good, or does it jump later?" Intro rates, first-year discounts, promotional anything.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 8</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> the deal exists only when it's been said back to you. Complete it:<br><br>"Could you read that ___ to me — price, contract, end date?"</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"Could you read that back to me — price, contract, end date?"</b><br>After the haggling, the audit. Making <i>them</i> read the deal back catches everything a happy customer misses: the contract length that reset, the exit fee that appeared, the extra that survived. The three-item list tells them exactly what you're checking — and out came a fact nobody had mentioned: twelve months, no exit fee.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "Could you read that back to me — ___, ___, ___?" Name the fields you care about; make the deal recite itself.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 9</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> evidence before the line goes dead. Complete it:<br><br>"And can I get that confirmed by ___ before we ___ ___?"</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"And can I get that confirmed by email before we hang up?"</b><br><b>Before we hang up</b> is the whole point. "It'll be in your next bill" and "the system sends it automatically" are where phone deals go to dissolve — and a promised email that never arrives is unprovable by teatime. Stay on the line, watch it land, thank them warmly. Thirty extra seconds; sixteen pounds a month.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "Can I get that in writing before we finish?" Every verbal agreement, everywhere, always.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 10</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> closing so the human on the headset had a good call too. Say it out loud, then write it.</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="Sixteen pounds a month saved, email in your inbox. Last line to Darren…"></textarea>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal a strong version</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>e.g. "You've made that easy — I appreciate it."</b><br>Darren didn't set the pricing policy; he walked you down the ladder as fast as his screen allowed. Naming that — "made it easy" — costs nothing and is simply accurate. And notice what warmth bought at the end of this call: the free masterclass. <i>Ask for retentions. Say the magic words. Most callers don't have the other price ready.</i> People tell things to callers they liked.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "You've made that easy — I appreciate it." For every gatekeeper who opened the gate.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="keep">
            <h3>🔑 Make them yours</h3>
            <p>These ten will pay for themselves within the month. Say each answer out loud before you type it.</p>
            <p class="cue">"I've been with you ___ years, and ___ has just gone up again." — which of your real bills is this sentence already true of?</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="The bill, the years, the rise…"></textarea>
            <p class="cue">"___ are offering the same thing for ___." — go and find the real number for that bill. Write the sentence you'd say.</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="The rival, the price, in front of you…"></textarea>
            <p class="cue">"If that's really the best you can do…" — could you say it and mean it? What would you actually do if they said "okay, cancel"?</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="Your honest answer — and whether you'd follow through…"></textarea>
        </div>

        <a class="morelink" href="modern_life_phrase_bank.html">🧰 <span>Every phrase from every Modern Life lesson, sorted by what it does.</span><span class="go">Open →</span></a>

        <div class="tip"><b>Quick test:</b> run the ladder out loud — eight years, gone up again → not ringing to complain → in front of me → that's better, but → lose the extras → then I'd like to cancel → for good, or does it jump → read that back → email before we hang up → made that easy. Ten sentences; roughly two hundred pounds a year.</div>""",

