"""        <h2>The Echo <span class="em">🕰️</span></h2>

        <div class="echo-card">
            <p>In the East End of London in the 1960s, the Kray twins ran what they called protection. Shopkeepers and club owners paid a weekly amount, and in return nothing happened to their windows. The longer you'd paid, the more you paid — loyalty, in that economy, was measured and billed. And the one question nobody dared ask at collection time was the obvious one: <i>what exactly am I paying for?</i></p>
            <p>Everyone understood the real answer: for things to stay as they were. It worked until people stopped being afraid of the conversation — witnesses talked, the twins went down in 1969, and the weekly envelope turned out to have been optional all along, if enough people said no at once.</p>
            <p>Your broadband provider is not the Krays. But any bill that rises quietly each year, for things staying as they are, is charging you for the conversation you haven't had. The difference is that your conversation is safe, takes twenty minutes, and ends with an email and sixteen pounds a month. Have it.</p>
        </div>

        <a class="morelink" href="short_history_the_kray_twins.html">👔 <span>Want the full story? The twins, the clubs, and the empire of quiet payments…</span><span class="go">Open →</span></a>

        <h3>Two bigger questions</h3>
        <ol class="bigq">
            <li>The Krays' customers paid to avoid a conversation. What do you currently pay — in money, time or discomfort — to avoid one?</li>
            <li>Is haggling with a call centre fair game or wasted life? Where's the line between a good deal and a second job?</li>
        </ol>""",
